NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4000

CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:4001
API_URL=http://localhost:4000/api

# Base de Datos (Neon o Local)
# Si la URL no trae `sslmode`, se completa según NODE_ENV: verify-full en
# production, disable en cualquier otro caso (pensado para Postgres local).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://usuario:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require

# Correr las migraciones pendientes automáticamente al iniciar. El código
# usa true por defecto (ausente = true), que es lo correcto en un deploy:
# arrancar es un acto deliberado.
#
# En LOCAL déjalo en false. Con `nest start --watch`, arrancar no es
# deliberado - basta guardar un archivo para que el servidor recompile y
# migre. Eso ya ocurrió una vez sobre producción (2026-08-29). Con false,
# las migraciones solo se aplican corriendo `pnpm migration run` a mano.
#
# En producción (Render, imagen Docker) se deja en TRUE a propósito, y
# puesto de forma explícita en el dashboard aunque true ya sea el default:
# con una sola instancia, migrar al arrancar es correcto y no hay un paso
# que se pueda olvidar. Un paso de deploy aparte tampoco protege de una
# migración mala - también corre sin supervisión; lo único que compra es
# que un fallo tumbe el deploy en vez de dejar el contenedor nuevo en
# crash-loop.
#
# Ponlo en false en producción solo si escalas a más de una réplica, para
# que dos contenedores arrancando a la vez no compitan por aplicar la
# misma migración. En la imagen Docker `pnpm migration run` NO existe
# (ts-node es devDependency, los fuentes TS no se copian y el Dockerfile
# borra npm/npx/corepack del runtime); el comando que sí funciona es:
#
#   node node_modules/typeorm/cli.js migration:run -d dist/database/data-source.js
MIGRATIONS_RUN_ON_BOOT=false

# Auth Clerk
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=tu_secret_aqui

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH DATABASE SHOULD .env POINT AT?
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A development branch, not production. Local development runs real code
# against whatever this says: migrations, one-off scripts, a mistaken DELETE.
# On 2026-08-29 a migration reached production purely because `nest start
# --watch` rebuilt after the file was written and applied it on boot, and six
# envelopes lost their category as a result.
#
# MIGRATIONS_RUN_ON_BOOT=false (above) closes that specific path, and the
# env-file fix in data-source.ts closed the actual root cause (a bare
# config() meant a local test run loaded THIS file). The general one stays
# open while this points at real user data. Neon branches are cheap: make
# one for development the same way .env.test already has its own, and let
# production be reached only by a deploy.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DESTRUCTIVE MIGRATIONS: SPLIT THEM ACROSS TWO DEPLOYS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the rule that actually protects production, and it holds whether
# migrations run on boot or as a deploy step.
#
# Render deploys without downtime: the new container starts before the old
# one stops. So for one window the OLD code serves traffic against the NEW
# schema. A migration that drops or renames something the old code still
# reads breaks it while it's still answering requests.
#
# Roughly one in three migrations here is destructive in up() (see
# envelope_category_fk, add_currency_to_budget_and_expense), so this is not
# hypothetical. Split them:
#
#   Deploy 1 - add the new column/table, stop writing to the old one,
#              backfill. Nothing is removed, so both versions of the code
#              work against this schema.
#   Deploy 2 - drop the old column.
#
# No deploy then has code running against a schema missing something it
# uses. It also covers scaling past one replica.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TESTS (.env.test)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `pnpm test:e2e` runs with NODE_ENV=test, which makes both
# src/config/app-config.module.ts and src/database/data-source.ts read
# `.env.test` instead of this file. Create it (it is gitignored) with the same
# keys as above.
#
# Its DATABASE_URL must point at a DEDICATED database - a separate Neon branch
# or project is enough, and it may keep the same database name. This is
# enforced, not a suggestion: test/assert-safe-test-database.ts compares the
# target against this file's DATABASE_URL and aborts the run if they are the
# same database. The e2e suites execute `DELETE FROM expense` and
# `DELETE FROM envelope` between tests, so reusing the everyday database
# destroys real data - and pasting that URL in here is the easiest possible
# mistake to make, which is exactly what that check exists to catch.
